.TH CSVTUI 1 "2026-08-07" "csvtui 0.3.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
csvtui \- terminal viewer for CSV files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B csvtui
[\fIoptions\fR] \fIfile.csv\fR
.br
\fIcommand\fR | \fBcsvtui\fR [\fIoptions\fR] \fB\-\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B csvtui
displays a CSV file as an aligned grid in the terminal and lets you move around
it with vim-style keys. The file is read lazily, so only a window of rows is
resident in memory at any time.
.PP
Quoted fields containing the delimiter or newlines, escaped double quotes,
CRLF line endings and a leading byte order mark are all handled. Column widths
are measured in terminal cells, so accented and double-width text aligns.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-delimiter " " \fIchar\fR
Field delimiter. Accepts \fBtab\fR or \fB\\t\fR for tab-separated files.
Auto-detected from the first line when not given.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-header
Treat the first row as data rather than as column names.
.TP
.B \-\-header
Force the first row to be a header. This is the default.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B h j k l
Move the cursor. Arrow keys work too.
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-D ", " Ctrl\-U
Half page down or up.
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-F ", " Ctrl\-B ", " PgDn ", " PgUp
Full page down or up.
.TP
.BR gg ", " G
Jump to the first or last row. \fIn\fBG\fR jumps to row \fIn\fR.
.TP
.BR 0 ", " $
First or last column.
.TP
.B /
Search forward. Search is smart case: an all-lowercase pattern matches
case-insensitively. Searches wrap around the end of the file.
.IP
A search runs on a worker thread, since a pattern that is absent takes as long
as reading the whole file. While it runs the grid is dimmed and no key but
.B Esc
is accepted: a search produces a cursor position, so moving the cursor while it
looks for one has no meaning.
.TP
.BR n ", " N
Next or previous match.
.TP
.B f
Filter rows to those matching a pattern. An empty pattern clears the filter.
.TP
.BR s ", " S
Sort by the cursor column, ascending or descending. Numeric columns sort
numerically.
.TP
.B u
Clear the sort and the filter.
.TP
.BR x ", " X
Hide the cursor column, or show all columns again.
.TP
.B z
Freeze the columns up to the cursor so they stay visible when scrolling right.
.TP
.B Enter
Show the full value of the current cell.
.TP
.B y
Copy the current cell to the system clipboard using OSC 52, which works over
SSH.
.TP
.B c
Show statistics for the cursor column: row count, empty count, and min, max and
mean for numeric columns.
.TP
.B H
Pin or unpin the header row.
.TP
.B t
Toggle between aligned and raw modes.
.TP
.B ?
Toggle the help overlay.
.TP
.B q
Quit. \fBEsc\fR closes an overlay first, and clears the search highlight.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
The file could not be read.
.TP
.B 2
The command line was invalid.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CSVTUI_MEMORY_LIMIT
Bytes that csvtui may consider available when deciding whether an operation
will fit in memory. Overrides what the system reports. Useful on shared
machines, and for testing the guard. A lower limit shrinks the sort buffer,
which means more spilling rather than a refusal.
.TP
.B CSVTUI_TMPDIR
Directory for the temporary runs a large sort writes. Falls back to
.B TMPDIR
and then to
.BR /tmp .
Worth setting when the default is a small tmpfs and the file being sorted is
not small.
.TP
.B CSVTUI_CACHE_DIR
Where chunk offset tables are kept between sessions. Falls back to
.I $XDG_CACHE_HOME/csvtui
and then to
.IR ~/.cache/csvtui .
.SH LARGE FILES
Browsing does not depend on the size of the file: opening reads about a thousand
rows and stops, and scrolling stays at a few megabytes of resident memory.
.PP
Everything that must read every row \(em
.BR G ,
.IB n G
past the end, sorting, filtering and column statistics \(em runs on a worker
thread, reports progress in the status bar and stops on
.BR Esc .
The grid stays scrollable throughout. Each is a single pass: sorting a file
whose length is not yet known no longer counts it first and then sorts it.
.PP
Until the file has been read the status bar shows a
.B ~
prefixed row estimate derived from the file size. Once counted, the chunk
offsets are retained \(em and written to the cache directory, so reopening the
same file starts with an exact count and nothing read. Searching and scrolling
never trigger a count.
.PP
Only the column being sorted or summarised is parsed out of each record, rather
than every field of every row.
.PP
A sort fills a bounded buffer with keys, sorts it, writes it to a temporary
file as a run, and merges the runs at the end, so its memory does not follow
the size of the file: what remains is the resulting order, at one row number
per row. Runs are removed when the sort finishes or is cancelled.
.PP
Filtering holds roughly 10 bytes per row. csvtui estimates that cost up front
and refuses, with numbers, rather than exhausting memory.
.SH NOTES
Once started, sorting and filtering make one full pass over the file, so on a
multi-gigabyte file they take a while. All other operations stream.
.PP
Sorting places numbers before text in both directions, so empty and
non-numeric cells collect at the end whether the sort is ascending or
descending. Rows with equal keys keep their order in the file.
.PP
Column statistics describe the rows currently visible, so an active filter
applies to them; a sort does not, since it reorders the same set.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR column (1),
.BR csvlook (1)
